{{- /*
  Skips connection tracking for WireGuard traffic between servers and desks:
  the UDP packets on WireGuard's port (inventory vars.wireguard_listen_port)
  to or from the public address of every inventory node that has one. Read
  by roles/every_server and roles/systems_desk as a template, loaded by
  wireguard-udp-443-redirect.service after the redirect, and written empty
  by server_management_scripts/server_first_boot.cloud-config.yml.

  Why: any NAT table on the host, the redirect's included, gives every new
  flow a "null NAT binding". When two peers send first packets at the same
  moment, such as after a wg0 reload, or a peer that learned port 51820 meets
  a flow redirected from 443, the tracking tuples clash and NAT rewrites the
  source port to a random one. WireGuard roams to that port. When the entry
  expires (120 s idle, or the conntrack -D drop-in of wg-quick@wg0.service),
  the peer sits on a dead port, and between servers, which send no keepalive,
  it stays there: 12+ minutes, measured on test servers. An untracked packet never
  reaches NAT. Servers and desks therefore use Endpoint
  <public_ipv4_address>:51820 for each other (no redirect in their path) and
  skip tracking here. Only an Endpoint change failed alone (0/10); with this
  table all 12 directed pairs of 4 nodes answered after 150 s of silence,
  through three rounds that included a wg0 reload on every node.

  Who lists the addresses: a server renders them here from the inventory,
  so an ordinary every_server run adds a new server or desk. A desk renders
  an include of /etc/systems-desk/wireguard-notrack-addresses.nft instead,
  which only update-this-desks-wireguard-peers writes (from pushed main, with
  the server peers). Reason: a desk review must show no change when a server
  joins or leaves (the desk's wg0 follows the same rule), so the desk's
  rendered files cannot list the servers.

  Laptops are not in the set: they keep UDP 443 and the redirect, and a
  laptop's PersistentKeepalive keeps its flow alive.

  Order on an existing system: this table and the Endpoint change reach
  machines one run at a time, and any mix still carries traffic.
    - A packet to the redirected port 443 (a peer not yet moved) is not
      matched here: dport is 443, not 51820. It is tracked and redirected
      as before.
    - A packet to 51820 from a peer that this machine does not yet untrack
      is tracked as before, and 51820 is open in every firewall since the
      earlier version. An untracked packet has ct state "untracked", not
      "invalid", so no firewall rule drops it, and ufw and the desk's
      firewall admit 51820 with no state condition.
    - A reply leaves this machine untracked from port 51820. The peer that
      sent to 443 accepts it: WireGuard takes the packet on its own socket
      whatever the tracking says, the firewall admits 51820, and WireGuard
      roams the peer to <address>:51820, which is where it should end.
  So no step of the move can leave a pair unable to talk, and no step
  closes a port.
*/ -}}
#!/usr/sbin/nft -f
# Replace only our table, so loading this file again is safe.
table inet stack_wireguard_notrack {}
flush table inet stack_wireguard_notrack
table inet stack_wireguard_notrack {
    set server_and_desk_public_addresses {
        type ipv4_addr
    }
    chain prerouting {
        type filter hook prerouting priority raw; policy accept;
        ip saddr @server_and_desk_public_addresses udp dport {{ .inputs.wireguard_listen_port }} notrack
    }
    chain output {
        type filter hook output priority raw; policy accept;
        ip daddr @server_and_desk_public_addresses udp sport {{ .inputs.wireguard_listen_port }} notrack
    }
}
{{- if .inputs.is_systems_desk }}
include "/etc/systems-desk/wireguard-notrack-addresses.nft"
{{- else }}
{{- range $name, $node := .inputs.inventory_nodes }}
{{- $public := get $node.vars "public_ipv4_address" | default "" }}
{{- if $public }}
add element inet stack_wireguard_notrack server_and_desk_public_addresses { {{ $public }} }
{{- end }}
{{- end }}
{{- end }}
